            <ArticleTitle>Changes in atherogenic lipids and lipoproteins during natural and hyperstimulated cycles in healthy women.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>231-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare changes in the concentrations of Lp(a) lipoprotein and other atherogenic lipids and lipoproteins in natural and hyperstimulated ovarian cycles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Open, prospective study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Academic department in a teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Normal ovulatory women served as controls and the treatment group consisted of women undergoing ovarian hyperstimulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">The treatment group received hMG on a fixed regimen.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Measurements of serum lipids and lipoproteins were made in both natural and hyperstimulated cycles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was a significant increase in the median concentration of Lp(a) lipoprotein from 10.1 to 10.7 mg/dL in the luteal compared with the proliferative phase of unstimulated cycles. In hyperstimulated cycles, there was a significant increase in the median concentration of Lp(a) lipoprotein from 9.7 mg/dL in the proliferative phase to 11.3 mg/dL in the luteal phase. Reductions in concentrations of total cholesterol and low-density lipoprotein cholesterol were found in the luteal phase of hyperstimulated cycles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Concentrations of Lp(a) lipoprotein increase during the luteal phase of both natural and hyperstimulated ovarian cycles.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
